{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_51","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"caption\":\"Case v. Montana\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2026-01-14\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"607 U.S. ___; 146 S. Ct. 500; No. 24-624\",\"proposition\":\"Emergency-aid entry requires an objectively reasonable basis for believing an occupant is seriously injured or imminently threatened; probable cause is not required. Brigham City's formulation applies without added gloss, and emergency aid should not be mislabeled community caretaking after Caniglia.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"emergency aid; community caretaking boundary\",\"discovery_path\":\"SCOTUS/emergency-aid search -> opinion: https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-624/\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Victor Javier Grandia Gonzalez\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-07-19\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"107 F.4th 1304; No. 23-10578\",\"proposition\":\"Joined the circuit consensus that the Fourth Amendment does not categorically require a misdemeanor to occur in an officer's presence before a warrantless public arrest; probable cause supported the arrest and ensuing backpack search incident to arrest.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"search incident to arrest; validity of predicate warrantless arrest\",\"discovery_path\":\"SITA/warrantless-misdemeanor-arrest search -> published opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca11/23-10578/23-10578-2024-07-19.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Gonzalez v. United States\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2025-02-24\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-5577, cert. denied\",\"proposition\":\"Justice Sotomayor, joined by Justice Gorsuch, identified a serious historical challenge to the circuits' rejection of a Fourth Amendment in-the-presence rule for warrantless misdemeanor arrests and urged review in a suitable case; this was a certiorari statement, not a merits holding.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"search incident to arrest; predicate arrest; emerging historical limitation\",\"discovery_path\":\"follow-up from Eleventh Circuit Gonzalez -> SCOTUS statement and docket: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/24-5577 ; https://www.supremecourt.gov/search.aspx?filename=/docket/docketfiles/html/public/24-5577.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Gilbert Perez\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the First Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-08-23\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"89 F.4th 247; No. 22-1121, rehearing en banc denied\",\"proposition\":\"Denial of rehearing left intact the 2023 panel holding that Robinson's categorical person-search rule permits searching a backpack worn at arrest even after police secure it. The accompanying statement foregrounded the acknowledged split with the Fourth Circuit's Davis and Tenth Circuit's Knapp approaches to carried containers.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"search incident to arrest; physical containers; circuit split\",\"discovery_path\":\"SITA/backpack split and rehearing search -> order and statement: https://caselaw.findlaw.com/court/us-1st-circuit/116520126.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Turner\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-12-04\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 22-4055\",\"proposition\":\"Held that Gant's reasonable-to-believe-evidence-of-the-arrest-offense standard for a vehicle search incident to arrest requires less than probable cause; the court did not decide whether it equals Terry reasonable suspicion.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"search incident to arrest; vehicle; Gant evidentiary prong\",\"discovery_path\":\"Gant/reasonable-to-believe circuit search -> published opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/22-4055/22-4055-2024-12-04.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Pinder\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-11-26\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-4154\",\"proposition\":\"A genuine driver's license remained evidence relevant to the false-identification offense of arrest under Gant even after officers independently established the driver's identity, so the vehicle search incident to arrest was valid.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"search incident to arrest; vehicle; Gant evidentiary nexus\",\"discovery_path\":\"Gant/relevance-of-evidence search -> circuit opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca10/23-4154/23-4154-2024-11-26.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Allen\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-04-28\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-4604\",\"proposition\":\"Davis remained controlling that Gant's reach-distance restriction applies to nonvehicle bags once an arrestee is secured, although the government preserved a Supreme Court challenge. Suppression was nevertheless reversed because mandatory, discretion-limiting police and jail inventory policies made discovery of the bag contents inevitable; a policy may be proved by testimony rather than a written copy.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"search incident to arrest; carried bags; inventory; inevitable discovery\",\"discovery_path\":\"SITA-container split and inventory search -> published opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/24-4604/24-4604-2026-04-28.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Hays\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-01-12\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"90 F.4th 904; No. 22-3294\",\"proposition\":\"A passenger's drugs and pipe, combined with evidence connecting the car to a trafficking location, supplied probable cause to search the vehicle and every area or container capable of concealing the suspected contraband; ownership by driver or passenger did not delimit the automobile exception.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"automobile exception; passenger contraband; containers\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 automobile-exception search -> published opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca7/22-3294/22-3294-2024-01-12.html\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Morley\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-04-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"99 F.4th 1328; No. 22-12988\",\"proposition\":\"Applied the automobile exception to a search performed by a government-controlled informant who entered the defendant's car and retrieved a briefcase at a coconspirator's direction; the totality supplied probable cause, making it unnecessary to rely on apparent authority.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"automobile exception; government agent; container search\",\"discovery_path\":\"automobile-exception/state-agent search -> published opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca11/22-12988/22-12988-2024-04-30.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Perry\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-02-06\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 21-4684\",\"proposition\":\"A girlfriend had actual authority to consent to a phone search where she had been its sole user for about seven months and exercised joint access or control, despite the defendant's ownership of the device.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"consent; third-party authority; cellular phone\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 third-party-consent search -> published opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/21-4684/21-4684-2024-02-06.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Briscoe\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-04-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-4013\",\"proposition\":\"Upheld a finding of voluntary implied consent where the resident calmly answered officers and stepped back in a manner permitting entry; consent may be manifested by conduct rather than express words. The ensuing protective sweep was also upheld on the record.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"consent; implied consent; protective sweep\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 implied-consent search -> published opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/23-4013/23-4013-2024-04-30.html\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Olson v. County of Grant\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-02-10\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"127 F.4th 1193; No. 23-35365\",\"proposition\":\"Reviewing a previously created full forensic phone extraction is itself a Fourth Amendment search governed by Riley. Limited consent to one agency's investigation did not authorize another agency's later, unrelated review; the search violated the Fourth Amendment, though qualified immunity applied because the rule was not yet clearly established.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"consent scope; digital extraction; Riley limitation on SITA\",\"discovery_path\":\"consent/forensic-copy search -> published opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca9/23-35365/23-35365-2025-02-10.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Payne\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-04-17\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"99 F.4th 495; No. 22-50262\",\"proposition\":\"California's general parole-search condition authorized a warrantless phone search despite ambiguity created by an added special condition, and Riley's SITA warrant rule did not govern parole searches. Compelling a thumb to unlock the phone was non-testimonial for Fifth Amendment purposes because it required no cognitive disclosure.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"special needs; parole search; cellular phone; Fifth Amendment biometric unlocking\",\"discovery_path\":\"parole/special-needs and compelled-unlock search -> published opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca9/22-50262/22-50262-2024-04-17.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Harden\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-06-18\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"104 F.4th 830; No. 20-14004\",\"proposition\":\"Joined the Ninth Circuit in holding that an otherwise reasonable probation search of a probationer's home is not invalid merely because a knowledgeable nonprobationer co-occupant lives there or objects; such a co-occupant assumes a diminished privacy expectation, and Randolph's consent rule does not control.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"special needs; probation search; nonprobationer co-occupant; consent boundary\",\"discovery_path\":\"probation-search/co-occupant split search -> published opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca11/20-14004/20-14004-2024-06-18.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Augustine Perez\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-02-23\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-7280\",\"proposition\":\"A supervised-release condition covering the supervisee's property did not authorize searching a house he owned but leased to a third-party tenant. Officers needed probable cause—not reasonable suspicion—to believe the supervisee resided in the third party's home before invoking his search condition; landlord ownership and an objecting tenant distinguish cohabitation cases such as Harden.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"special needs; supervised-release search; third-party residence; consent analogy\",\"discovery_path\":\"probation-search/third-party-home search -> published opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/23-7280/23-7280-2026-02-23.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Wells v. Fuentes\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-01-22\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-1638\",\"proposition\":\"In qualified-immunity litigation, upheld a vehicle inventory conducted before a necessary tow under standard procedures and treated minor deviations or leaving some items in the vehicle as insufficient to make the inventory investigatory. It also treated an officer's initial public-place welfare inquiry as permissible community-caretaking activity.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"inventory; vehicle impoundment; community caretaking\",\"discovery_path\":\"inventory/community-caretaking search -> published opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/23-1638/23-1638-2025-01-22.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Javarese Holmes\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-06-23\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-10794\",\"proposition\":\"An inventory search is not all-or-nothing: an officer may stop the inventory after discovering a firearm and seek a warrant rather than continue rummaging. Truncating the procedure in this less-intrusive manner does not establish pretext or invalidate what was already found.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"inventory; partial or discontinued inventory; pretext\",\"discovery_path\":\"inventory-search completion/pretext search -> published opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca11/23-10794/23-10794-2025-06-23.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Villarreal\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-12-12\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"Nos. 24-40525 and 24-40527\",\"proposition\":\"Evidence was admissible through inevitable discovery where, before the alleged misconduct, the officer had decided under standard practice to impound the unlicensed driver's vehicle and the ensuing inventory was imminent. An unwritten policy and limited officer discretion did not defeat the showing where historical facts established the normal practice.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"inventory; inevitable discovery; impoundment discretion\",\"discovery_path\":\"inventory/inevitable-discovery search -> circuit opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca5/24-40525/24-40525-2025-12-12.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Giambro\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the First Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-01-15\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"126 F.4th 46; No. 24-1052\",\"proposition\":\"Rejected emergency aid where officers lacked an objectively reasonable basis to believe the supposedly deceased woman was alive, inside, and in need of help; the court also rejected any freestanding dead-body or murder-scene exception and faulted officers for ignoring immediately available people who could clarify the situation.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"emergency aid; limits; no crime-scene exception\",\"discovery_path\":\"emergency-aid limiting-case search -> published opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca1/24-1052/24-1052-2025-01-15.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Cannon v. Filip\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-12-31\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"Nos. 24-2655 and 24-3113\",\"proposition\":\"A detailed 911 report of an ongoing, violent domestic assault, together with the volatility and potentially lethal character of domestic violence, supplied an objectively reasonable emergency basis for warrantless home entry even though the report reached police through family intermediaries.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"emergency aid; domestic violence; 911 information\",\"discovery_path\":\"emergency-aid/domestic-violence search -> circuit opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca7/24-2655/24-2655-2025-12-31.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Maxwell\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2023-11-13\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"85 F.4th 1243; No. 22-2135\",\"proposition\":\"Pre-2024 but newly doctrine-shaping in later emergency-aid cases: visible bullet holes and occupants' silence supported an objectively reasonable belief that someone inside might be injured; silence does not necessarily dispel an emergency and may reinforce it.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"emergency aid; gunfire; occupant silence\",\"discovery_path\":\"recent Cannon/Case-progeny review traced repeated reliance to the 2023 published opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca7/22-2135/22-2135-2023-11-13.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Newman v. Underhill\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-04-23\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"134 F.4th 1025; No. 24-1493\",\"proposition\":\"A nine-minute pause did not break hot pursuit where officers immediately chased a fleeing felon, retained a reasonably good idea of his location, and continuously acted with speed while awaiting backup and searching. Continuity turns principally on loss of the suspect's trail, continued speedy efforts, and elapsed time.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"exigency; hot pursuit; Lange progeny; continuity\",\"discovery_path\":\"Lange/hot-pursuit continuity search -> published opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca9/24-1493/24-1493-2025-04-23.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Jones v. City of North Las Vegas\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-03-06\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"150 F.4th 1030; No. 24-3374, amended opinion and rehearing en banc denied\",\"proposition\":\"Hot pursuit had ended when officers lost a misdemeanor suspect's trail in a residential neighborhood for at least eighteen minutes; a later K-9 alert might establish probable cause but could not revive the extinguished exigency. The amended opinion survived an en banc vote over a multi-judge dissent disputing both qualified-immunity specificity and the panel's post-Lange treatment of misdemeanor gravity.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"exigency; hot pursuit; Lange progeny; terminated continuity; en banc denial\",\"discovery_path\":\"follow-up from Newman -> amended published opinion and en banc order: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca9/24-3374/24-3374-2026-03-06.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Bakutis v. Dean\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-02-25\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"129 F.4th 299; No. 24-10271\",\"proposition\":\"Granted qualified immunity for a welfare-response perimeter or curtilage sweep without deciding a broad merits exception. After Caniglia, community-caretaking functions must be evaluated separately rather than treated as a standalone home-entry license, but existing precedent did not clearly establish that the officer's limited conduct was unlawful.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"community caretaking; curtilage; Caniglia; qualified-immunity limitation\",\"discovery_path\":\"post-Caniglia community-caretaking search -> published opinion: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca5/24-10271/24-10271-2025-02-25.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"McMurry v. Weaver\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-06-27\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-50571\",\"proposition\":\"A child-welfare investigation was not sufficiently divorced from general law enforcement to invoke special needs or community caretaking for opening a family's refrigerator; absent a court order or consent, officers needed an objectively reasonable basis for immediate danger. The unlawfulness was clearly established, so qualified immunity was denied.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"special needs; community caretaking; emergency aid; child-welfare investigation\",\"discovery_path\":\"child-welfare/special-needs/community-caretaking search -> official published opinion: https://www.ca5.uscourts.gov/Opinions/pub/24/24-50571-CV0.pdf\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"}}
